        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#who-makes-it" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Who Makes It</a>
          <a href="#label" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The ISO100 Label</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Pebbles Range</a>
          <a href="#which-to-buy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Which One to Buy</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Cost per 25 g</a>
          <a href="#colors-sweeteners" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Colors and Sweeteners</a>
          <a href="#other-cereal-flavors" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Other Cereal Flavors</a>
          <a href="#how-to-use" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. How to Use It</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Who Should Skip It</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Fruity Pebbles protein powder is not a novelty tub from an unknown seller. It is Dymatize ISO100, one of the best-known whey isolates in the US, wearing a cereal license, and the label holds up: about 25 g of protein, roughly 1 g of sugar and around 120 calories per scoop. The real decisions are which Pebbles product fits your goal, whether the sweeteners and colors bother you, and how much you are paying for the flavor.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="who-makes-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Who Actually Makes Fruity Pebbles Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Fruity Pebbles protein powder you see in grocery aisles, at Target and on Amazon is made by <strong>Dymatize</strong>, under license from Post Consumer Brands, which owns the Pebbles cereal line. Dymatize sells the flavor across its ISO100 whey isolate and its Super Mass Gainer, and it also lists a Fruity Pebbles version of its ready-to-drink Performance Protein Shake. If a tub says Fruity Pebbles and protein powder on the front, it is almost certainly one of these.
          </p>

          <p>
            The family connection explains the partnership. Dymatize is owned by BellRing Brands, the company that also owns Premier Protein. BellRing was spun out of Post Holdings, the parent of the Pebbles cereal business, with the main distribution to Post shareholders completed in March 2022 and Post selling its remaining BellRing shares later that year. The two companies are now separate, but the license has stayed in place and the Pebbles flavors remain on Dymatize's current product pages.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Pebbles range is broader than most people realize. Alongside Fruity Pebbles, Dymatize's site lists ISO100 in <strong>Cocoa Pebbles</strong> and <strong>Pebbles Birthday Cake</strong>, all three built on the same hydrolyzed whey isolate base with near-identical macros. The flavor you pick changes the taste and color of the shake, not the nutrition, so the choice between them is purely about which cereal you liked as a kid.
          </p>

          <p>
            One common mix-up is worth clearing up. Pure Protein, a different company, sells Fruity Pebbles and Cocoa Pebbles <em>protein bars</em> with about 20 g of protein each. Those are bars, not powder, and they come with more calories and a chewy coating. If you searched for the powder and landed on a bar listing, that is why. This page covers the powders and the shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the flagship is ISO100, everything you know about that product applies here. It is a hydrolyzed and regular whey isolate, which means very little lactose, fat or carbohydrate, and it mixes thin in water. For a full look at the rest of the Dymatize range, see our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize protein powder review</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Is on the ISO100 Fruity Pebbles Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Per Dymatize's own nutrition panel at the time of writing, one 30 g scoop of ISO100 Fruity Pebbles delivers <strong>25 g of protein, 120 calories, 2 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of total sugar with 0 g added, and 1 g of fat</strong>. Some US grocery listings show 110 calories for the same tub, which reflects rounding and label revisions rather than a different formula. Either way, about 83 percent of the scoop by weight is protein, which is excellent for a heavily flavored powder.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">ISO100 Fruity Pebbles, per 30 g scoop (label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">120</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Total sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2.6 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Leucine</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The protein comes from hydrolyzed whey protein isolate and whey protein isolate. Dymatize lists 5.5 g of branched-chain amino acids per scoop, including about 2.6 g of leucine (one retailer listing rounds it to 2.7 g). That leucine figure matters because roughly 2.5 g per meal is the amount most often cited for maximally switching on muscle protein synthesis in younger adults, and one scoop clears it on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the ingredient list is short: less than 2 percent of natural and artificial flavors, salt, soy lecithin, and two sweeteners, <strong>sucralose and steviol glycosides</strong> (stevia). The allergen statement lists milk and soy. The scoop also carries about 120 mg of sodium and 120 mg of calcium. There are no gums, no added creamer and no added sugar, which is why the calorie count stays so low.
          </p>

          <p>
            On testing, Dymatize's Fruity Pebbles page describes the product as banned-substance tested but does not name the certifier on that page. If you compete in a tested sport, look up the exact flavor in the Informed Choice or Informed Sport database before you buy rather than assuming every ISO100 flavor and tub size is covered. For everyone else, the more useful point is that the label is clean and the protein figure is from isolate, not padded with added amino acids.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Pebbles Range, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
