        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Pebbles Product Should You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For nearly everyone, the answer is <strong>ISO100 Fruity Pebbles</strong>. It gives you 25 g of protein for about 120 calories, so it works in a cut, a maintenance phase or a lean bulk, and it is easy on most stomachs because isolate carries very little lactose. Buy the 20-serving tub first if you have never tried the flavor, then move to the 5 lb tub once you know you like it, because the larger size usually brings the cost per scoop down noticeably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick <strong>ISO100 Cocoa Pebbles</strong> if you would rather have a chocolate shake that tastes like cereal milk than a fruity one. The macros are the same, with 25 g of protein and about 120 calories per scoop per the label, and the flavor is a little easier to pair with coffee, banana or peanut butter. Choose <strong>Pebbles Birthday Cake</strong> if you like frosting-style dessert flavors; it is the sweetest-sounding of the three but carries the same 1 g of sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            The <strong>Super Mass Gainer Fruity Pebbles</strong> is a completely different product and only suits a narrow group. One serving is 2.5 cups, or 333 g of powder, and delivers about 1,280 calories, 52 g of protein and 245 g of carbohydrate per Dymatize's label. That is useful for a hard gainer who cannot eat enough food, and a mistake for anyone else. If you are trying to gain weight, read our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">best protein powder for weight gain</a> guide before committing to a 6 lb bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ready-to-drink <strong>Dymatize Performance Protein Shake in Fruity Pebbles</strong> is the grab-and-go option. Dymatize lists 30 g of protein, 160 calories and 1 g of sugar per 11.5 fl oz bottle, made from milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate and sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. It costs more per gram of protein than the powder, but it is handy for a gym bag or a desk drawer, and it does not need a shaker.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you specifically want a fruity cereal shake with a different base or brand, the main alternative right now is Ghost Whey in <strong>Trix Cereal Milk</strong>, launched with General Mills in March 2026. It is a whey blend rather than a pure isolate, so it is slightly creamier and slightly higher in calories. It is a reasonable swap if you find Dymatize too thin, and it appears as the alternative card in the picks above.
          </p>
        </div>

