        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Fruity Pebbles protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dymatize makes it under license from Post Consumer Brands, which owns the Pebbles cereals. The flavor appears in Dymatize ISO100 whey isolate, Super Mass Gainer and the Performance Protein Shake. Dymatize is owned by BellRing Brands, the company behind Premier Protein, which was spun out of Post Holdings and completed its separation in 2022. Other brands sell cereal flavors, but not the Pebbles license.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein is in Fruity Pebbles protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                ISO100 Fruity Pebbles lists 25 g of protein per 30 g scoop, with about 120 calories, 2 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of sugar, per Dymatize's label at the time of writing. Some retailer listings show 110 calories for the same product. The mass gainer version is very different, at about 52 g of protein and 1,280 calories per 333 g serving.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Fruity Pebbles protein powder healthy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The ISO100 version has a strong nutrition profile: mostly protein, very little sugar or fat, and over 2.5 g of leucine per scoop. It does contain sucralose, stevia, artificial flavors and color additives, which some people prefer to avoid. For most healthy adults it is a reasonable supplement. Anyone with a medical condition, pregnancy or kidney disease should ask a doctor or dietitian first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Fruity Pebbles protein powder contain Red 3?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It has historically used color additives, and Dymatize's product page says ingredient statements may vary while it transitions to a formula without Red No. 3. The FDA revoked Red No. 3 for food in January 2025 and gave manufacturers until January 2027 to reformulate. If this matters to you, check the ingredient list on the tub you receive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Fruity Pebbles protein good for weight loss?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                ISO100 Fruity Pebbles suits a calorie deficit well because it delivers 25 g of protein for about 120 calories and roughly 1 g of sugar, which helps you hit a protein target without many extra calories. The mass gainer does the opposite and should be avoided when cutting. Protein powder supports weight loss only as part of an overall calorie deficit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I bake with Fruity Pebbles protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. It works in pancakes, muffins, mug cakes, yogurt bowls and no-bake bites. Because whey isolate dries out quickly, replace only part of the flour, add extra liquid or yogurt, and shorten the bake slightly. Heat can mute the flavor and change the texture, but cooking does not reduce the protein's nutritional value in any way that matters.
              </p>
